   "use": "The general 'I'm not feeling well', usually softened to chotto guai warui; others ask guai warui no? or guai dou? (how are you feeling?).",
   "notes": "The sets next to it split the job. Taichou ga warui means the same in a more careful word, and it's the safe phrase for calling in sick at work. Choushi ga warui is being off form, and also a machine acting up (pasokon no choushi ga warui). Kibun ga warui is feeling sick or faint right now (in a car, in a crowd). Kimochi warui (in the senses list) is queasy, but also 'gross, creepy'.",
